WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.180
- Can I let you guys
in on a little secret?

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.590
I really want our show to be a safe space.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:09.040
A place where we can leave
our differences at the door

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.210
and just feel welcomed.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:16.250
This is an all ages party
and everyone's invited.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.790
Our dream with this show
is to find common ground,

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:20.410
or as I call it,

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.580
the pretty little middle,
#prettylittlemiddle.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.930
And no matter what your
beliefs or values are today,

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.990
I wanted to celebrate Ruth Bader Ginsburg.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:33.000
And I found all these things
as I dove in this weekend,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.500
I read everything.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.780
I read her book.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.560
I watched her documentary.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.890
I immersed myself in her life.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.550
Her marriage was just so amazing,

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.060
the way her and her husband
supported each other.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.000
When he was sick,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.040
she took care of him and did his homework.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.210
When she was sick,

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:51.390
he took care of her.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.580
They were so supportive of each other,

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.090
and she just had this incredible strategy

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.940
about the long game and

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.360
she had this ability to be
confident and comfortable

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.760
in silence as she talks about in her book.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.000
I learned something, I'm horrible at that.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.740
Her attitude about anger,
pushing people away,

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.110
that I learned from too, you know?

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:17.220
She was a law student
and a judge and a lawyer

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.850
and she has this incredible legacy.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:23.420
And now she inspires a
whole new generation.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.220
And, I kind of thought after

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.660
being able to ace any test
you could give me on RBG,

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.150
what can I talk about on the
show about her, you know?

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:37.710
We're all seeing
everything all weekend and

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.780
I was compelled to talk about

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.720
how she found common ground

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.370
with all her colleagues
on the Supreme Court,

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.370
especially when they didn't agree.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.610
And they didn't argue small fights.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:52.130
It wasn't like, "Did you
see the game last night?"

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:53.890
Or "I swear to God,

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.860
if you park your car
near my driveway again"

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.720
or "What's better, the
French fry or tater tot?"

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.930
Like, they are confronting
the biggest issues.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:06.170
And things are all easy when we agree.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.530
But when we don't, can we find the way in

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.410
where we might come together?

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.310
I know I am naive in thinking this, but

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.810
she was able to maintain friendships

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.430
with colleagues who she disagreed with.

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.730
And it just moved me so much.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.140
And I thought, you know what?

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.970
I wanna shine the light on this.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.230
And I found the lyrics to this opera.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.420
They were written for
her and Antonin Scalia.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.760
And they were like on opposite
sides of the spectrum.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.940
And they still found a way to
have an incredible friendship.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:37.080
And when I read the words to this,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.330
I had an incredible revelation.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.460
That even though they
felt like they represented

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.710
the divides and attitudes and values,

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.350
they could still work together.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:47.581
And it gave me so much hope.

00:02:47.581 --> 00:02:50.164
(upbeat music)

